
I implemented both pathfinding algorithms — Dijkstra and A* ,because each plays a different but important role in NAVEX.

Dijkstra does not use coordinates.
It explores the graph uniformly and always produces the guaranteed optimal path in any weighted map.
It is reliable even if the input data (like node positions) is incomplete or inaccurate.

A* uses coordinates and a Euclidean heuristic to guide the search toward the destination.
This makes A* significantly faster and more efficient, especially in a city-map environment where node positions are meaningful.

In NAVEX, A* is the primary algorithm for real-time navigation because it is faster and more practical for large road networks.
However, in situations where:

coordinate data is missing,

coordinates are unreliable or noisy, or

a fallback method is needed for verification,

NAVEX will switch to Dijkstra to ensure correctness and robustness.

This dual-algorithm system provides both high performance and high reliability, similar to how real navigation systems handle pathfinding.
